    <s:String x:Key="HelpTranscode">The Transcode tab encodes files that are already downloaded. It does not change the download options. Add files and Add folder fill the list. Include subfolders takes nested folders. A folder named transcoding is skipped while scanning. Clear removes the list while encoding is not running.&#10;&#10;Check the files to encode. The header check box checks or clears every row. Each row shows the duration, the resolution, the codecs, the audio bitrate, the size, the estimated new size and the saving. A file that is still downloading, and an incomplete .part or .ytdl file, is left out of the batch.&#10;&#10;Save folder sets one destination for the whole batch. Default writes each result into a transcoding subfolder next to the source. The source file stays in place.&#10;&#10;Above the preset list, choose the video codec (copy, H.264, HEVC, VP9, AV1 or audio only), the resolution from the original down to 144p, the CRF quality from 18 to 35, the audio codec (copy, AAC, Opus, MP3 or FLAC), the audio bitrate from 64 to 320 kbps, and the container. A lower CRF keeps more detail and makes a larger file. Copying the video disables the resolution and the CRF. FLAC and copied audio disable the bitrate list. Changing any of these controls selects the Custom preset. VP9 and AV1 take longer than H.264. The container list includes MP4, MKV, MOV, M4V, 3GP, AVI, MPG, MPEG-TS, WMV and WebM; an audio-only result uses the matching audio container.&#10;&#10;The built-in presets are As-is, Compatible, Smaller, Strong, audio-only Opus at 64 kbps, audio-only MP3 at 128 kbps, and Custom. The table compares the current size, the estimate, the difference and the saving. The percent is an estimate, not the exact size after encoding. The list opens with the built-in presets, then the saved presets in name order.&#10;&#10;A custom preset is stored with Save, replaced with Update and removed with Delete. Built-in presets cannot be deleted, and As-is cannot be saved. An empty name is rejected. A name may contain up to 80 characters. If the name already exists, the program asks whether to replace that preset.&#10;&#10;Before a batch starts, one question covers every file that already has a result: skip those files or transcode them again. The question separates a matching signature from a file that exists with different settings. Transcode again suppresses the question and always writes the result anew.&#10;&#10;Transcode starts the batch. Pause stops the whole batch: the file that was encoding starts from the beginning when work resumes, finished files keep the Done state, and files that have not started wait. Cancel stops the batch and deletes the temporary files. The simultaneous-encode limit changes the number of running encodes at once.&#10;&#10;While a file is encoding, its row shows the elapsed encode time. The batch line shows the elapsed time, the estimated time left, the estimated total time and the finish date. When the batch finishes, a message says that the job is finished and how long it took. The same sentence remains under the progress bar.&#10;&#10;The job list keeps batches. Delete job removes the record and leaves the video files on disk. An unfinished batch can continue after the program closes. An interrupted file is encoded from the beginning, and finished files are not overwritten unless the skip-or-retranscode question says so.</s:String>
